#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e6a96fc8-6ce5-5f47-8c20-709754ed7bc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5811" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the graph of $y =2x^3- 6 x^2 +8$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2050/Source/Images/406c751f6da68203b0244bc247f45bf0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient ($a_3 = 2$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$ as as $x$ approaches $-\\infty$.\n- $P(x)$ approaches $\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty$.\n\nOnly the graph $\\textbf{C}$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128896 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7098a9a5-23ea-5496-a492-191c9c1a0690" :question/math-academy-id "e-5815" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials is shown in the diagram above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2050/Source/Images/3d8aad2c552ec02fb6ff1d648981e0ad.png)\n\n1. $-2 x^4 + 6 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 6 x + 4$\n2. $-2 x^5 + 6 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x + 1$\n3. $3 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 4$\n4. $-x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty$, and\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty$.\n\nConsequently,\n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, $P(x)$ is neither II nor III.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $4$, which means that $P(0)$ must be equal to $4$.\n\nTherefore, $P(x)$ is given by polynomial I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128897 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "fdba6918-c9be-5a77-b79c-4a5cf39bf6e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5817" :question/problem "The graph of a polynomial $Q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2050/Source/Images/99486ce0777e1af063fed9015c9cf382.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n2. The degree of $Q(x)$ is odd.\n3. The constant term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $Q(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty$, and\n- $Q(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nConsequently,\n\n- the degree of $Q(x)$ is odd, and\n- the leading coefficient of $Q(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo I is false, and II is true.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $0$, which means that $Q(0)$ must be equal to $0$, and therefore the constant term must be equal to $0$. So, III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0864251d-271d-5b31-a031-2142aba788c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242925} {:answer/id #uuid "e731c2c2-cee1-5cea-9515-38dfee280a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242926} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d13358-049d-51a7-ad4e-3ab46fccfe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242927} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd92169-241f-57f7-b6ca-c570614b87b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/t